<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Usrushana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ushrusana, Osrushana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Usrushana was a historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, in modern northern Tajikistan. Ruled by local princes with the title afshin, it was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> in the early 9th century. Its best-known son was the general al-Afshin, who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajid-dynasty">Sajid dynasty</a> also came from its military families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usrushana.jpg" alt="Usrushana" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bunjikat,Kahkaha1-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The region, also called Ustrushana, lay south of the great southernmost bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and stretched roughly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a>; its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunjikat">Bunjikat</a>. It had close historical, cultural, ethnic and linguistic ties to Sogdia, of which it at first formed part, before developing its own identity as the area became more urbanised. The exact form of its Iranian name is unclear from the sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 5th to the 7th century the region was part of the territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, and after 560 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>, though the principality, ruled directly by its afshins, probably kept some autonomy. The first Muslim invasion, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a>, did not bring it under control. Nasr ibn Sayyar subdued it incompletely in 739, the afshin made a nominal submission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, and in 794, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a>, the ruling afshin Karākana submitted to al-Fadl ibn Yahya of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakids</a>.</p><p>Karākana's son Kavus withdrew his allegiance, and during a power struggle in the ruling family his son Khaydar sought refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. In 822 Khaydar guided an Abbasid army into the region; Kavus abdicated and Khaydar succeeded him, later becoming one of the great nobles of the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mutasim, where he was known as al-Afshin. He fought the Khurramites under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babak-khorramdin">Babak Khorramdin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, but in 841 he was arrested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> on suspicion of plotting against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>.</p><p>After his death the region became Islamic; Khaydar had preserved its temples from ruin. His dynasty continued to reign until 893, and a coin of its last ruler from 892 is in the Hermitage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>. The region then became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, though semi-autonomous afshins may have continued to rule there, and its Iranian population was eventually almost entirely replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osrushana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>One Sogdian dialect survives: Yaghnobi, thought to descend from the speech of the principality of <b>Ushrusana</b>, is still spoken by the Yaghnobis of Tajikistan.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sajids were descended from a military family from <b>Ushrusana</b> in Central Asia, in the service of the caliphs.</blockquote><a href="/state/sajid-emirate">Sajid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
